# Chapter 3 — FreeIPA Identity, Certificates & LDAP Integration

## What FreeIPA Actually Is

A domain controller. All nodes are added to it so they trust it as the authority. On it I create users,
host groups, user groups, and rules. Creating users and groups alone does nothing — no access is granted
until rules are created on top.

---

## The 3 Layers — This Is NOT Like AWS

AWS combines everything into one policy document. FreeIPA splits access control into 3 completely
separate systems:

**Layer 1 — HBAC (Host-Based Access Control) = the door.**
Can this user SSH to this machine? Yes or no, nothing else. k8s_admin has HBAC rule for k8s_masters →
can SSH in. No HBAC rule for vault nodes → SSH rejected at PAM level before even asking for a password.
This is authentication — getting through the door.

**Layer 2 — Sudo Rules = what you can do after you're in.**
No sudo rule = stuck as a regular Linux user. Can read world-readable files, run non-root commands, but
can't touch system config, restart services, change firewall rules, install packages. super_bot has
`!authenticate` flag → passwordless sudo on automation_group nodes. k8s_admin has sudo with password
prompt on k8s nodes. This is authorization — what you're allowed to do inside.

**Layer 3 — IPA Roles = can this user manage FreeIPA itself.**
Create users, reset passwords, modify DNS. Not relevant to my setup — only admin manages IPA, and admin
is hardcoded as superuser. Doesn't need roles assigned in the UI. Think of it like root on Linux — root
doesn't need to be in sudoers, it just IS root by design.

---

## The Mental Model Difference From AWS

AWS = deny all by default. New user can do nothing. You build UP from zero.

Linux/FreeIPA = allow all by default for logged-in users. You fence DOWN. Any user who gets through the
door (HBAC) is immediately a regular Linux user — can run any non-root command, read world-readable
files, write to /tmp and home directory. The fencing is: HBAC controls which machines they can reach,
sudo rules control what they can do as root, and that's it.

If you need to restrict even non-root commands (like limiting a monitoring user to only ps and top), the
option is rbash (restricted bash) — bash with hard limits: can't cd, can't change PATH, can't run
commands with / in them, can't redirect output. Set their shell to `/bin/rbash` and control their PATH.
In practice rarely done because "no sudo + HBAC scoping to specific hosts" is sufficient for most cases.
The dangerous operations (shutdown, reboot, ip link set, nmcli modify) already require root — no sudo
rule = they can't run them anyway.

