## SSSD and Cache Timing

SSSD (System Security Services Daemon) runs on every domain-joined node. It does two different things at
different times:

**At login (each SSH attempt):**
- Validates password against FreeIPA live
- Checks HBAC against FreeIPA live
- Caches user info (UID, GID, groups, home dir)

**On a schedule (independent of logins):**
- Pulls sudo rules from FreeIPA's LDAP every ~15 minutes
- Full refresh every 6 hours

So sudo rules are already cached on the node before anyone logs in. When a user runs sudo, the system
checks the local SSSD cache — not FreeIPA in real time.

**What this means for revocation:**

| What changed on IPA | When node knows | Session impact |
|---|---|---|
| Sudo rule removed | ~15 min (SSSD refresh) | User keeps sudo until cache expires |
| HBAC rule removed | Next SSH attempt | Existing sessions stay alive |
| Password changed | Immediately (checked live) | No session impact |
| User deleted | ~15 min (SSSD refresh) | Existing session stays until timeout |

To force immediate effect: `sssctl cache-expire -E` or `systemctl restart sssd` on the node.

