## Vault ↔ FreeIPA Integration (LDAP)

LDAP (Lightweight Directory Access Protocol) is the protocol external services use to query FreeIPA's
user and group directory. FreeIPA runs 389 Directory Server under the hood — LDAP is how Vault talks to
it.

**The flow when a user logs into Vault:**

```
User enters vault_operator + password in Vault UI
  → Vault's LDAP auth backend sends BIND request to FreeIPA (port 636)
  → FreeIPA validates the password → YES
  → Vault queries FreeIPA: what groups is vault_operator in?
  → FreeIPA returns: vault-admins
  → Vault checks its LOCAL mapping:
      vault write auth/ldap/groups/vault-admins policies=super_admin
  → Vault issues token with super_admin policy
```

FreeIPA knows nothing about Vault policies. It only answers two questions: is the password correct, and
what groups is this user in. All authorization happens inside Vault. The `vault write auth/ldap/groups`
mapping is stored in Vault — it's Vault's own config, not IPA's.

The vault-admins group in FreeIPA has no special attributes, no rules, no policies attached to it. It's
just a group name. Vault is the one that gives that group name meaning by mapping it to the super_admin
policy.

**FreeIPA = phone book. Vault = the building that calls the phone book to verify who you are, then
applies its own rules inside.**

---

## Certificate Coverage Summary

| Connection | Who signs the cert | Why trust works |
|---|---|---|
| Vault nodes ↔ each other (Raft) | FreeIPA CA | All vault nodes have /etc/ipa/ca.crt |
| K8s vault-injector → Vault API | FreeIPA CA | K8s nodes are domain-joined |
| K8s nodes ↔ each other (API server, kubelet) | K8s internal CA (kubeadm) | Separate PKI, unrelated to FreeIPA |

